{"name":"Ocean Wave","hex":"#7dbcaa","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ocean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the large bodies of water separating the continents.","The continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocean"},{"term":"wave","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move back and forth repeatedly and somewhat loosely.","To move one's hand back and forth (generally above the shoulders) in greeting or departure."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A moving disturbance in the level of a body of liquid; an undulation.","The ocean."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wave"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ocean Wave","blocks":[{"text":"Ocean Wave was an American Thoroughbred racehorse who was a main rival of\nCount Fleet in the early 1940s. Owned by Calumet Farm, he was trained by Ben Jones and owned by Warren Wright, Ocean Wave was the son of Blenheim\nand Columbiana. He developed into a come from behind threat as a two-year-old and a three-year-old."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_Wave","wikidata":"Q7076116"},{"title":"Ocean Wave (1891 steamboat)","blocks":[{"text":"Ocean Wave was a steamboat that was operated from 1891 to 1897 on the Columbia River, from 1897 to 1899 on Puget Sound and from 1899 to 1911 as a ferry on San Francisco Bay. Ocean Wave is perhaps best known for transporting summer vacationers from Portland, Oregon to seaside resorts near Ilwaco, Washington during its service on the Columbia River. This vessel is also known for being the first ferry placed in service by the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe Railway."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/54/Ocean_Wave_at_Portland_circa_1894.jpg/330px-Ocean_Wave_at_Portland_circa_1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_Wave_(1891_steamboat)","wikidata":"Q18154806"},{"title":"Wind wave","blocks":[{"text":"In fluid dynamics, a wind wave, or wind-generated water wave, is a surface wave that occurs on the free surface of bodies of water as a result of the wind blowing over the water's surface. The contact distance in the direction of the wind is known as the fetch. Waves in the oceans can travel thousands of kilometers before reaching land. Wind waves on Earth range in size from small ripples to waves over 30 m (100 ft) high, being limited by wind speed, duration, fetch, and water depth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Porto_Covo_Outubro_2014-3.jpg/330px-Porto_Covo_Outubro_2014-3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_wave","wikidata":"Q165848"},{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs, and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harboring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean","wikidata":"Q9430"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Ocean_Wave_%28sidewheeler%29.jpg/330px-Ocean_Wave_%28sidewheeler%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Ocean_Wave_%28sidewheeler%29.jpg/330px-Ocean_Wave_%28sidewheeler%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ocean Wave, in a drawing published in 1894, clearly based on the leading photograph in this article.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOcean_Wave_(sidewheeler).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/White_Collar_Line_ad_1895.jpg/500px-White_Collar_Line_ad_1895.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/White_Collar_Line_ad_1895.jpg/500px-White_Collar_Line_ad_1895.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Advertisement published August 18, 1895, showing Ocean Wave as part of the White Collar Line.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhite_Collar_Line_ad_1895.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/83/Ocean_Wave_tied_up_%28pre-1900%29.jpg/330px-Ocean_Wave_tied_up_%28pre-1900%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/83/Ocean_Wave_tied_up_%28pre-1900%29.jpg/330px-Ocean_Wave_tied_up_%28pre-1900%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ocean Wave prior to 1900, apparently out of service.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOcean_Wave_tied_up_(pre-1900).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Ocean_Wave_%28sidewheeler%29_02.jpg/500px-Ocean_Wave_%28sidewheeler%29_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Ocean_Wave_%28sidewheele